sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/lewontins-paradox-of-variation--claude-opus-5-5--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Decompose π into N_e and μ using pedigree mutation rates" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/lewontins-paradox-of-variation> ;
    dct:description "Part of the paradox may sit in μ, not N_e: the drift-barrier hypothesis predicts that per-generation mutation rate falls as N_e rises, which flattens π = 4N_eμ against N_c. Pedigree-based germline mutation rates now exist for dozens of vertebrates (Bergeron et al. 2023). For every species with both a pedigree μ and a published π (Romiguier et al. 2014, Leffler et al. 2012, genome-scale estimates in NCBI-linked studies), compute N̂_e = π/(4μ) and fit log N̂_e on log N_c (PGLS). Pre-registered predictions: if the paradox is entirely in N_e, then log μ is uncorrelated with log N_c and the slope of log N̂_e equals the slope of log π; if drift-barrier compression matters, log μ declines with log N_c and the N̂_e slope is steeper than the π slope by the μ slope. The quantity of interest is the fraction of the gap between neutral slope 1 and the observed π slope that μ accounts for." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "The estimated slope of log μ on log N_c and the implied share of the π–N_c gap explained by mutation-rate variation, with 95% CI. If the share's interval excludes 0, μ is a quantified part of the answer; if it is centred near 0 with a tight interval, the paradox is entirely in N_e." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Fewer than about 30 species with both pedigree μ and comparable π, giving intervals spanning 0 to 100%: inconclusive, underpowered." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/data-test" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Pre-register the decomposition and predicted signs; list admissible μ estimates (trio-based, with stated callable-genome correction). 2. Assemble the matched set (species with both μ and π); resolve taxa via the organisms layer. 3. Fit PGLS for log π, log μ and log N̂_e on log N_c; propagate μ uncertainty by parametric bootstrap. 4. Report the share of the paradox attributable to μ with its 95% interval." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: phylogenetic-comparative-methods" ;
    skos:note "Tool: organisms-layer" ;
    skos:note "Tool: data-test" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/kimura-heterozygosity> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/kimura-neutral-theory> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/kimura-substitution-rate> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-05752-y> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/vsbuffalo/paradox_variation> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-05752-y> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/nrg.2016.104> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13685> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/nrg.2016.58> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/lewontins-paradox-of-variation> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Lewontin's paradox of variation" .

  <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-05752-y> dct:title "Bergeron et al. 2023 germline mutation rates (supplementary tables)" ;
    dct:description "Pedigree-based per-generation mutation rates across vertebrates" .
  <https://github.com/vsbuffalo/paradox_variation> dct:title "Buffalo 2021 compilation" ;
    dct:description "π estimates and census proxies to match against" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-05752-y> dct:title "Evolution of the germline mutation rate across vertebrates" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Bergeron et al. 2023, Evolution of the germline mutation rate across vertebrates" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1038/nrg.2016.104> dct:title "Genetic drift, selection and the evolution of the mutation rate" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Lynch et al. 2016, Genetic drift, selection and the evolution of the mutation rate" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13685> dct:title "Comparative population genomics in animals uncovers the determinants of genetic diversity" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Romiguier et al. 2014, Comparative population genomics in animals uncovers the determinants of genetic diversity" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1038/nrg.2016.58> dct:title "Determinants of genetic diversity" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Ellegren and Galtier 2016, Determinants of genetic diversity" .
}

